</table></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.F5.2.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">(a) Partial-label supervision​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​</p>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="A2.F5.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_bottom" style="width:115.3pt;"><img src="2510.03016v1/assets/figure/generate_figure_grid/imagenette_Vanilla_SSL_1_grid_10x10.png" id="A2.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:143/143;" width="143" height="143" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="A2.F5.3.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">(b) Suppl-unlabeled supervision</p>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="A2.F5.4" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_bottom" style="width:103.3pt;"><img src="2510.03016v1/assets/figure/generate_figure_grid/imagenette_Vanilla_NLL_sym_40_grid_10x10.png" id="A2.F5.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:143/143;" width="143" height="143" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="A2.F5.4.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">(c) Noisy-label supervision</p>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Examples of randomly generated ImageNette images from <span id="A2.F5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Vanilla</span> models trained under different types of imprecise supervision.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It has been established <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vincent, 2011</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song &amp; Ermon, 2019</a>)</cite> that these two formulations differ only by an additive constant independent of <math id="A2.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ESM}}(\theta;q(X,Y))=\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{DSM}}(\theta;q(X,Y))+C_{3}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ESM</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>DSM</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ESM}}(\theta;q(X,Y))=\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{DSM}}(\theta;q(X,Y))+C_{3},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on <math id="A2.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, both objectives admit the same minimizer.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Applying this result to an imprecise-label dataset by identifying <math id="A2.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=Z</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A2.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}_{\mathrm{ESM}}:=\arg\min_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ESM}}(\theta;q(X,Z))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>ESM</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ESM</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}_{\mathrm{ESM}}:=\arg\min_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ESM}}(\theta;q(X,Z))</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the optimal score function satisfies <math id="A2.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{\theta^{*}_{\mathrm{ESM}}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},z,t)=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}\!\mid\!Z=z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>ESM</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∣</mo></mpadded><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{\theta^{*}_{\mathrm{ESM}}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},z,t)=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}\!\mid\!Z=z)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the same conclusion holds for <math id="A2.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{DSM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>DSM</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{DSM}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain <math id="A2.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{\theta^{*}_{\mathrm{ESM}}}=\mathbf{s}_{\theta^{*}_{\mathrm{DSM}}}=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}\!\mid\!z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>ESM</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>DSM</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∣</mo></mpadded><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{\theta^{*}_{\mathrm{ESM}}}=\mathbf{s}_{\theta^{*}_{\mathrm{DSM}}}=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}\!\mid\!z)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is precisely the statement of Remark <a href="#Thmremark1" title="Remark 1. ‣ 3.2 Generative Objective: Modeling the Imprecise Data Distribution ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To directly illustrate this bias, we train CDMs under different forms of imprecise supervision by applying Eq. (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.2 Generative Objective: Modeling the Imprecise Data Distribution ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) directly, a baseline we refer to as <span id="A2.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Vanilla</span>.
We then visualize the images generated by these biased models, as shown in the figures below.
The results reveal the following patterns:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="A2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Partial-label supervision:</span> The generated images often lack diversity and typically capture only the dominant object. This effect is particularly pronounced on the ImageNette dataset, where samples within the same class appear highly similar. Interestingly, the generated categories generally align with the ground-truth labels, suggesting that diffusion models can still extract correct class information under partial-label supervision. However, the inherent label ambiguity prevents the model from capturing intra-class variation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Noisy-label supervision:</span> The generated samples tend to contain visual noise. Although the model is able to capture class diversity, corrupted labels cause mismatches between generated samples and their true categories.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Supplementary-unlabeled supervision:</span> The generated images are often both less diverse and noisier. This phenomenon combines the limitations of partial-label supervision with the challenge of abundant unlabeled samples. Because the model has limited access to labeled examples, it relies on averaging confidence across all classes, which reduces its discriminative boundaries and introduces noise.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.6 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3.2 Generative Objective: Modeling the Imprecise Data Distribution ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></h3>

<div id="A2.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, for all <math id="A2.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the perturbed distribution <math id="A2.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|z)</annotation></semantics></math> satisified:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|z)=\sum_{y=1}^{c}p(y|z)q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|y)\quad\forall\mathbf{x}_{t}\in\mathcal{X},z\subset\mathcal{Y}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>c</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|z)=\sum_{y=1}^{c}p(y|z)q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|y)\quad\forall\mathbf{x}_{t}\in\mathcal{X},z\subset\mathcal{Y}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This implies that the transition from imprecise labels to clean labels is independent of the timesteps. Consequently, Eq. (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 3.2 Generative Objective: Modeling the Imprecise Data Distribution ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) can be derived as follows,</p>
<table id="A2.Ex43" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex43X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex43X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|z)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex43Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex43Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
