<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex43Xi.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex43Xi.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{y=1}^{c}p(y|\mathbf{x}_{t},z)\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>c</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{y=1}^{c}p(y|\mathbf{x}_{t},z)\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}|y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.7 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 3.2 Generative Objective: Modeling the Imprecise Data Distribution ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></h3>

<div id="A2.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By Remark <a href="#Thmremark1" title="Remark 1. ‣ 3.2 Generative Objective: Modeling the Imprecise Data Distribution ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3.2 Generative Objective: Modeling the Imprecise Data Distribution ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the optimal solution <math id="A2.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}_{\textnormal{Gen}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mtext>Gen</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}_{\textnormal{Gen}}</annotation></semantics></math> to Eq. (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In 3.2 Generative Objective: Modeling the Imprecise Data Distribution ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) satisfies</p>
<table id="A2.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{y=1}^{c}p(y\!\mid\!\mathbf{x}_{t},z)\,\mathbf{s}_{\theta^{*}_{\textnormal{Gen}}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},y,t)=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}\!\mid\!z)=\sum_{y=1}^{c}p(y\!\mid\!\mathbf{x}_{t},z)\,\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}\!\mid\!y)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>c</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mpadded style="width:0.356em;" width="0.356em"><mi>y</mi></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true">∣</mo></mpadded><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mtext>Gen</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∣</mo></mpadded><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>c</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mpadded style="width:0.356em;" width="0.356em"><mi>y</mi></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true">∣</mo></mpadded><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∣</mo></mpadded><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{y=1}^{c}p(y\!\mid\!\mathbf{x}_{t},z)\,\mathbf{s}_{\theta^{*}_{\textnormal{Gen}}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},y,t)=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}\!\mid\!z)=\sum_{y=1}^{c}p(y\!\mid\!\mathbf{x}_{t},z)\,\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}\!\mid\!y),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for all <math id="A2.SS7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\subseteq\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\subseteq\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Next, recall the weighted denoising score matching loss:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\textnormal{Gen}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{t}\!\left[w_{t}\,\Big\|\sum_{y=1}^{c}p(y\!\mid\!\mathbf{x}_{t},z)\,\mathbf{s}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},y,t)-\sum_{y=1}^{c}p(y\!\mid\!\mathbf{x}_{t},z)\,\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}\!\mid\!y)\Big\|_{2}^{2}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Gen</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>c</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mpadded style="width:0.356em;" width="0.356em"><mi>y</mi></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true">∣</mo></mpadded><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>c</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mpadded style="width:0.356em;" width="0.356em"><mi>y</mi></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true">∣</mo></mpadded><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∣</mo></mpadded><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\textnormal{Gen}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{t}\!\left[w_{t}\,\Big\|\sum_{y=1}^{c}p(y\!\mid\!\mathbf{x}_{t},z)\,\mathbf{s}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},y,t)-\sum_{y=1}^{c}p(y\!\mid\!\mathbf{x}_{t},z)\,\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}\!\mid\!y)\Big\|_{2}^{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS7.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Differentiating with respect to <math id="A2.SS7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},y,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},y,t)</annotation></semantics></math> and setting the derivative to zero yields</p>
<table id="A2.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial}{\partial\mathbf{s}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},y,t)}\mathcal{L}_{\textnormal{Gen}}(\theta)=2w_{t}\,p(y\!\mid\!\mathbf{x}_{t},z)\left(\mathbf{s}_{\theta^{*}_{\textnormal{Gen}}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},y,t)-\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}\!\mid\!y)\right)=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Gen</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mpadded style="width:0.356em;" width="0.356em"><mi>y</mi></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true">∣</mo></mpadded><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mtext>Gen</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∣</mo></mpadded><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial}{\partial\mathbf{s}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},y,t)}\mathcal{L}_{\textnormal{Gen}}(\theta)=2w_{t}\,p(y\!\mid\!\mathbf{x}_{t},z)\left(\mathbf{s}_{\theta^{*}_{\textnormal{Gen}}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},y,t)-\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}\!\mid\!y)\right)=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="A2.SS7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, for any <math id="A2.SS7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.SS7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y\!\mid\!\mathbf{x}_{t},z)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mpadded style="width:0.356em;" width="0.356em"><mi>y</mi></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true">∣</mo></mpadded><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y\!\mid\!\mathbf{x}_{t},z)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the optimality condition implies</p>
<table id="A2.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{\theta^{*}_{\textnormal{Gen}}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},y,t)=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}\!\mid\!y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mtext>Gen</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∣</mo></mpadded><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{\theta^{*}_{\textnormal{Gen}}}(\mathbf{x}_{t},y,t)=\nabla_{\mathbf{x}_{t}}\log q_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}\!\mid\!y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS7.p3.2" class="ltx_p">In particular, under the partial-label learning setting, if <math id="A2.SS7.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y\!\mid\!\mathbf{x}_{t},z)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mpadded style="width:0.356em;" width="0.356em"><mi>y</mi></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true">∣</mo></mpadded><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y\!\mid\!\mathbf{x}_{t},z)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the loss does not depend on <math id="A2.SS7.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},y,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},y,t)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the equality can be established without loss of generality.
This completes the proof.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.8 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 4 Time Complexity Reduction ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></h3>

<div id="A2.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first consider the case where the timestep <math id="A2.SS8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled from a log-normal distribution, as defined in the EDM framework. Specifically,</p>
<table id="A2.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ln(\tau)\sim\mathcal{N}(P_{\mathrm{mean}},P_{\mathrm{std}}^{2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>mean</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>std</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ln(\tau)\sim\mathcal{N}(P_{\mathrm{mean}},P_{\mathrm{std}}^{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS8.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the parameters are set to <math id="A2.SS8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathrm{mean}}=1.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>mean</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathrm{mean}}=1.2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathrm{std}}=-1.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>std</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathrm{std}}=-1.2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Accordingly, the probability density function of <math id="A2.SS8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="A2.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\tau)=\frac{1}{\tau\,P_{\mathrm{std}}\sqrt{2\pi}}\exp\left(-\frac{(\ln\tau-P_{\mathrm{mean}})^{2}}{2P_{\mathrm{std}}^{2}}\right),\quad\tau&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>std</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>mean</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>std</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\tau)=\frac{1}{\tau\,P_{\mathrm{std}}\sqrt{2\pi}}\exp\left(-\frac{(\ln\tau-P_{\mathrm{mean}})^{2}}{2P_{\mathrm{std}}^{2}}\right),\quad\tau&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS8.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The corresponding cumulative distribution function (CDF) is denoted as:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\tau)=\frac{1}{2}\left[1+\operatorname{erf}\left(\frac{\ln\tau-P_{\mathrm{mean}}}{P_{\mathrm{std}}\sqrt{2}}\right)\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>erf</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>mean</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>std</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\tau)=\frac{1}{2}\left[1+\operatorname{erf}\left(\frac{\ln\tau-P_{\mathrm{mean}}}{P_{\mathrm{std}}\sqrt{2}}\right)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS8.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{erf}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>erf</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{erf}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the error function.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The median of this distribution <math id="A2.SS8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\text{mid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>mid</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\text{mid}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the value at which the CDF equals <math id="A2.SS8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="A2.SS8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\tau_{\text{mid}})=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>mid</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\tau_{\text{mid}})=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS8.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure that the selected subinterval allows signal-dominant early timesteps and noise-dominant later timesteps to complement each other, we require the cumulative probability mass on either side of the median to be equal.
Formally, for subinterval boundaries <math id="A2.SS8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(l,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(l,r)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.SS8.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=l+\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=l+\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, we enforce the following symmetry condition:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(r)-F(\tau_{\mathrm{mid}})=F(\tau_{\mathrm{mid}})-F(l)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>mid</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>mid</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(r)-F(\tau_{\mathrm{mid}})=F(\tau_{\mathrm{mid}})-F(l).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS8.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Rewriting this with <math id="A2.SS8.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=l+\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=l+\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> gives:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(l+\Delta)+F(l)=2F(\tau_{\mathrm{mid}})=1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>mid</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(l+\Delta)+F(l)=2F(\tau_{\mathrm{mid}})=1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS8.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS8.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This implicit equation defines the subinterval <math id="A2.SS8.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(l,l+\Delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(l,l+\Delta)</annotation></semantics></math> such that the cumulative probability mass is centered around the median of <math id="A2.SS8.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>. To compute the left boundary <math id="A2.SS8.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, we solve:</p>
<table id="A2.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=\operatorname{Solve}_{\tau}\left(F(\tau)+F(\tau+\Delta)-1=0\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Solve</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=\operatorname{Solve}_{\tau}\left(F(\tau)+F(\tau+\Delta)-1=0\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS8.p4.2" class="ltx_p">and set <math id="A2.SS8.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=l+\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=l+\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>.
The solution can be obtained using any standard root-finding algorithm, such as the Brent method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brent, 2013</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS8.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS8.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We then consider the DDPM setting, where the timestep <math id="A2.SS8.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly sampled from a fixed interval.
Specifically, we assume <math id="A2.SS8.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, whose CDF is given by</p>
<table id="A2.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\tau)=\tau" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\tau)=\tau</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS8.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Under this distribution, the symmetry condition in Eq. (<a href="#A2.E21" title="In B.8 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix B Proof ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>) simplifies to</p>
<table id="A2.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex54"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle l</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex54.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\operatorname{Solve}_{\tau}\left(F(\tau)+F(\tau+\Delta)-1=0\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Solve</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\operatorname{Solve}_{\tau}\left(F(\tau)+F(\tau+\Delta)-1=0\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex55"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\operatorname{Solve}_{\tau}\left(\tau+\tau+\Delta-1=0\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Solve</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\operatorname{Solve}_{\tau}\left(\tau+\tau+\Delta-1=0\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex56"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1-\Delta}{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1-\Delta}{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS8.p5.3" class="ltx_p">and thus <math id="A2.SS8.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=l+\Delta=\frac{1-\Delta}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=l+\Delta=\frac{1-\Delta}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This result implies that the optimal subinterval is symmetric around the midpoint of the distribution. In the special case where only a single timestep is used (i.e., <math id="A2.SS8.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>), the best estimate of the conditional ELBO occurs exactly at the median. As the sampled timestep deviates further from the midpoint, classification accuracy tends to degrade.
This observation aligns with the empirical findings of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2023)</a></cite>, who reported that classification accuracy is maximized near the median and declines towards the edges. Their use of evenly spaced timesteps centered around the median further supports our strategy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS9" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.9 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3 (Necessary Condition for Optimal Subinterval). ‣ 4 Time Complexity Reduction ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a></h3>

<div id="A2.SS9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For clarity, we abbreviate <math id="A2.SS9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbar(\tau,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbar(\tau,y)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A2.SS9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbar(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbar(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>, since the proof does not depend explicitly on <math id="A2.SS9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define the weighted integral of <math id="A2.SS9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbar" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbar</annotation></semantics></math> and the normalization factor over an interval <math id="A2.SS9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[l,r]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[l,r]</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="A2.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(l,r):=\int_{l}^{r}\hbar(\tau)\,p(\tau)\,\mathrm{d}\tau,\qquad Z(l,r):=\int_{l}^{r}p(\tau)\,\mathrm{d}\tau," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>l</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>l</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(l,r):=\int_{l}^{r}\hbar(\tau)\,p(\tau)\,\mathrm{d}\tau,\qquad Z(l,r):=\int_{l}^{r}p(\tau)\,\mathrm{d}\tau,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS9.p1.2" class="ltx_p">so that the local expectation can be written as <math id="A2.SS9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{\prime}=A(l,r)/Z(l,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{\prime}=A(l,r)/Z(l,r)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="A2.SS9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{\prime\prime}=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim p(\tau)}[\hbar(\tau)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{\prime\prime}=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim p(\tau)}[\hbar(\tau)]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the global expectation.
The squared error objective in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E16" title="In 4 Time Complexity Reduction ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) then becomes</p>
<table id="A2.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(l,r):=\left(\mu^{\prime}-\mu^{\prime\prime}\right)^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(l,r):=\left(\mu^{\prime}-\mu^{\prime\prime}\right)^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS9.p1.3" class="ltx_p">subject to the probability-mass constraint <math id="A2.SS9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(l,r)=\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(l,r)=\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We apply the method of Lagrange multipliers with</p>
<table id="A2.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(l,r,\lambda):=\left(\mu^{\prime}-\mu^{\prime\prime}\right)^{2}+\lambda\Big(\int_{l}^{r}p(\tau)\,\mathrm{d}\tau-\alpha\Big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><mi>l</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(l,r,\lambda):=\left(\mu^{\prime}-\mu^{\prime\prime}\right)^{2}+\lambda\Big(\int_{l}^{r}p(\tau)\,\mathrm{d}\tau-\alpha\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS9.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By the Leibniz rule, the derivatives of <math id="A2.SS9.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(l,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(l,r)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS9.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(l,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(l,r)</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the interval boundaries are</p>
<table id="A2.Ex60" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial A(l,r)}{\partial l}=-\hbar(l)\,p(l),\quad\frac{\partial A(l,r)}{\partial r}=\hbar(r)\,p(r),\qquad\frac{\partial Z(l,r)}{\partial l}=-p(l),\quad\frac{\partial Z(l,r)}{\partial r}=p(r)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial A(l,r)}{\partial l}=-\hbar(l)\,p(l),\quad\frac{\partial A(l,r)}{\partial r}=\hbar(r)\,p(r),\qquad\frac{\partial Z(l,r)}{\partial l}=-p(l),\quad\frac{\partial Z(l,r)}{\partial r}=p(r).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS9.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Hence, the derivatives of <math id="A2.SS9.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{\prime}=A/Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{\prime}=A/Z</annotation></semantics></math> are</p>
<table id="A2.Ex61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\mu^{\prime}}{\partial l}=\frac{p(l)}{Z(l,r)}\,(\mu^{\prime}-\hbar(l)),\qquad\frac{\partial\mu^{\prime}}{\partial r}=\frac{p(r)}{Z(l,r)}\,(\hbar(r)-\mu^{\prime})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\mu^{\prime}}{\partial l}=\frac{p(l)}{Z(l,r)}\,(\mu^{\prime}-\hbar(l)),\qquad\frac{\partial\mu^{\prime}}{\partial r}=\frac{p(r)}{Z(l,r)}\,(\hbar(r)-\mu^{\prime}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS9.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Differentiating <math id="A2.SS9.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> w.r.t. <math id="A2.SS9.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS9.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> gives</p>
<table id="A2.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex62"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\partial L}{\partial l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\partial L}{\partial l}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex62.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=2(\mu^{\prime}-\mu^{\prime\prime})\cdot\frac{p(l)}{Z(l,r)}(\mu^{\prime}-\hbar(l))-\lambda p(l)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=2(\mu^{\prime}-\mu^{\prime\prime})\cdot\frac{p(l)}{Z(l,r)}(\mu^{\prime}-\hbar(l))-\lambda p(l),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex63"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\partial L}{\partial r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\partial L}{\partial r}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex63.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=2(\mu^{\prime}-\mu^{\prime\prime})\cdot\frac{p(r)}{Z(l,r)}(\hbar(r)-\mu^{\prime})+\lambda p(r)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=2(\mu^{\prime}-\mu^{\prime\prime})\cdot\frac{p(r)}{Z(l,r)}(\hbar(r)-\mu^{\prime})+\lambda p(r).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS9.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Setting both derivatives to zero yields the necessary conditions</p>
<table id="A2.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2(\mu^{\prime}-\mu^{\prime\prime})(\mu^{\prime}-\hbar(l))=\lambda Z(l,r),\qquad 2(\mu^{\prime}-\mu^{\prime\prime})(\hbar(r)-\mu^{\prime})=\lambda Z(l,r)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2(\mu^{\prime}-\mu^{\prime\prime})(\mu^{\prime}-\hbar(l))=\lambda Z(l,r),\qquad 2(\mu^{\prime}-\mu^{\prime\prime})(\hbar(r)-\mu^{\prime})=\lambda Z(l,r).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS9.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Equating the two expressions gives</p>
<table id="A2.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{\prime}-\hbar(l)=\hbar(r)-\mu^{\prime}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\mu^{\prime}=\tfrac{1}{2}\big(\hbar(l)+\hbar(r)\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{\prime}-\hbar(l)=\hbar(r)-\mu^{\prime}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\mu^{\prime}=\tfrac{1}{2}\big(\hbar(l)+\hbar(r)\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS9.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Substituting back, we obtain</p>
<table id="A2.Ex66" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{l}^{r}p(\tau)\,\hbar(\tau,y)\,\mathrm{d}\tau=\frac{Z(l,r)}{2}\,\big(\hbar(l,y)+\hbar(r,y)\big),\qquad Z(l,r):=\int_{l}^{r}p(\tau)\,\mathrm{d}\tau." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>l</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>l</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{l}^{r}p(\tau)\,\hbar(\tau,y)\,\mathrm{d}\tau=\frac{Z(l,r)}{2}\,\big(\hbar(l,y)+\hbar(r,y)\big),\qquad Z(l,r):=\int_{l}^{r}p(\tau)\,\mathrm{d}\tau.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS9.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Equivalently, the necessary optimality condition is <math id="A2.SS9.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Err}(l^{*},r^{*},y)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Err</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Err}(l^{*},r^{*},y)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="A2.SS9.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(l^{*},r^{*})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(l^{*},r^{*})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, this is also equivalent to</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A2.Ex67" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Err}(l^{*},r^{*},y):=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim p(\tau\mid\tau\in[l^{*},r^{*}])}[\hbar(\tau,y)]\;-\;\tfrac{1}{2}\big(\hbar(l^{*},y)+\hbar(r^{*},y)\big)=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Err</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℏ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Err}(l^{*},r^{*},y):=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim p(\tau\mid\tau\in[l^{*},r^{*}])}[\hbar(\tau,y)]\;-\;\tfrac{1}{2}\big(\hbar(l^{*},y)+\hbar(r^{*},y)\big)=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS9.p9.1" class="ltx_p">This establishes the necessary condition for an optimal subinterval.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Disccusion</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Analysis of Early-Learning Regularization in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In 3.3 Classification Objective: Inferring Labels from Imprecise Signals ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>)</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The effectiveness of Eq. (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In 3.3 Classification Objective: Inferring Labels from Imprecise Signals ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) can be better understood by examining the form of its gradient.
For clarity, we restate the loss with the following notation: given a noisy-labeled input <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{x},\hat{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{x},\hat{y})</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote the model’s output probabilities as <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> and the corresponding EMA target as <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\hat{y}}\in\mathbb{R}^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\hat{y}}\in\mathbb{R}^{c}</annotation></semantics></math> be the one-hot vector corresponding to the noisy label <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the loss over the whole dataset <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(\mathbf{x}^{[i]},\mathbf{\hat{y}}^{[i]})\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(\mathbf{x}^{[i]},\mathbf{\hat{y}}^{[i]})\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed according to Eq. (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In 3.3 Classification Objective: Inferring Labels from Imprecise Signals ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) as</p>
<table id="A3.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{Cls}}^{\text{NL}}(\mathcal{D})=-\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\,\big\langle\operatorname{sg}(\mathbf{r}^{[i]}),\,\log f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})\big\rangle,\quad\mathbf{r}^{[i]}=\mathbf{\hat{y}}^{[i]}-\lambda\,\frac{f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})\odot\big(\delta^{[i]}\mathbf{1}-f_{\phi}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})\big)}{1-\delta^{[i]}}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Cls</mtext><mtext>NL</mtext></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>sg</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{Cls}}^{\text{NL}}(\mathcal{D})=-\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\,\big\langle\operatorname{sg}(\mathbf{r}^{[i]}),\,\log f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})\big\rangle,\quad\mathbf{r}^{[i]}=\mathbf{\hat{y}}^{[i]}-\lambda\,\frac{f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})\odot\big(\delta^{[i]}\mathbf{1}-f_{\phi}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})\big)}{1-\delta^{[i]}}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{[i]}=\langle f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]}),f_{\phi}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{[i]}=\langle f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]}),f_{\phi}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{sg}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{sg}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the stop-gradient operator, and <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\odot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\odot</annotation></semantics></math> is the Hadamard product.
By construction <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}^{[i]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}^{[i]}</annotation></semantics></math> is treated as a <em id="A3.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">constant</em> w.r.t. <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> due to the stop-gradient.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmlemma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\psi}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\psi}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the pre-softmax logits such that <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})=\operatorname{softmax}(\mathbf{\psi}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})=\operatorname{softmax}(\mathbf{\psi}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}))</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the loss in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In 3.3 Classification Objective: Inferring Labels from Imprecise Signals ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), the gradients are</span></p>
<table id="A3.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{Cls}}^{\mathrm{NL}}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})}{\partial\mathbf{\psi}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})}=f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})-\operatorname{sg}\big(\mathbf{r}^{[i]}\big),\quad\text{for each }i=1,\dots,n," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Cls</mi><mi>NL</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>sg</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for each </mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{Cls}}^{\mathrm{NL}}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})}{\partial\mathbf{\psi}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})}=f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})-\operatorname{sg}\big(\mathbf{r}^{[i]}\big),\quad\text{for each }i=1,\dots,n,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and, by the chain rule,</span></p>
<table id="A3.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{Cls}}^{\mathrm{NL}}(\mathcal{D})=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}J_{\mathbf{z}_{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})^{\top}\!\left[f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})-\operatorname{sg}\!\big(\mathbf{r}^{[i]}\big)\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Cls</mi><mi>NL</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.812em;" width="0.812em"><mi>sg</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{Cls}}^{\mathrm{NL}}(\mathcal{D})=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}J_{\mathbf{z}_{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})^{\top}\!\left[f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})-\operatorname{sg}\!\big(\mathbf{r}^{[i]}\big)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\mathbf{z}_{\theta}}(\mathbf{x})=\partial\mathbf{z}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})/\partial\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\mathbf{z}_{\theta}}(\mathbf{x})=\partial\mathbf{z}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})/\partial\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is the Jacobian of the logits w.r.t. the parameters.
Equivalently, expanding <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}^{[i]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}^{[i]}</annotation></semantics></math> gives</span></p>
<table id="A3.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{Cls}}^{\mathrm{NL}}(\mathcal{D})=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}J_{\mathbf{z}_{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})^{\top}\!\left[f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})-\mathbf{\hat{y}}^{[i]}+\lambda\,\operatorname{sg}\!\Big(\frac{f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})\odot\big(\delta^{[i]}\mathbf{1}-f_{\phi}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})\big)}{1-\delta^{[i]}}\Big)\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Cls</mi><mi>NL</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.812em;" width="0.812em"><mi>sg</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{Cls}}^{\mathrm{NL}}(\mathcal{D})=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}J_{\mathbf{z}_{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})^{\top}\!\left[f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})-\mathbf{\hat{y}}^{[i]}+\lambda\,\operatorname{sg}\!\Big(\frac{f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})\odot\big(\delta^{[i]}\mathbf{1}-f_{\phi}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})\big)}{1-\delta^{[i]}}\Big)\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">For any <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,\dots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,\dots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, let us first verify that <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}^{[i]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}^{[i]}</annotation></semantics></math> sums to 1.
With</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex68" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}^{[i]}=\mathbf{\hat{y}}^{[i]}-\lambda\,\frac{f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})\odot\big(\delta^{[i]}\mathbf{1}-f_{\phi}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})\big)}{1-\delta^{[i]}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}^{[i]}=\mathbf{\hat{y}}^{[i]}-\lambda\,\frac{f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})\odot\big(\delta^{[i]}\mathbf{1}-f_{\phi}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})\big)}{1-\delta^{[i]}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">we sum over classes and using <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]}),\mathbf{1}\rangle=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]}),\mathbf{1}\rangle=1</annotation></semantics></math> yields</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex69" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}^{\top}\mathbf{r}^{[i]}=1-\lambda\,\frac{\delta^{[i]}-\langle f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]}),f_{\phi}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})\rangle}{1-\delta^{[i]}}=1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}^{\top}\mathbf{r}^{[i]}=1-\lambda\,\frac{\delta^{[i]}-\langle f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]}),f_{\phi}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})\rangle}{1-\delta^{[i]}}=1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">so <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}^{[i]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}^{[i]}</annotation></semantics></math> lies on the simplex (hence Eq. (<a href="#A3.E22" title="In C.1 Analysis of Early-Learning Regularization in Eq. () ‣ Appendix C Disccusion ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>) is an ordinary cross-entropy with a fixed target).
Let <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\psi}^{[i]}=\mathbf{\psi}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\psi}^{[i]}=\mathbf{\psi}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})</annotation></semantics></math> be the logits and recall
<math id="A3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\log\!\operatorname{softmax}(\mathbf{\psi})}{\partial\mathbf{\psi}}=I-\operatorname{softmax}(\mathbf{\psi})\mathbf{1}^{\top}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.047em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\log\!\operatorname{softmax}(\mathbf{\psi})}{\partial\mathbf{\psi}}=I-\operatorname{softmax}(\mathbf{\psi})\mathbf{1}^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math>
For the per-sample loss
<math id="A3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{[i]}=-\langle\operatorname{sg}(\mathbf{r}^{[i]}),\log\operatorname{softmax}(\mathbf{\psi}^{[i]})\rangle," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{[i]}=-\langle\operatorname{sg}(\mathbf{r}^{[i]}),\log\operatorname{softmax}(\mathbf{\psi}^{[i]})\rangle,</annotation></semantics></math>
the derivative w.r.t. logits is</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex70" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\ell^{[i]}}{\partial\mathbf{\psi}^{[i]}}=\operatorname{softmax}(\mathbf{\psi}^{[i]})-\operatorname{sg}(\mathbf{r}^{[i]})=f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})-\operatorname{sg}(\mathbf{r}^{[i]})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>sg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>sg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\ell^{[i]}}{\partial\mathbf{\psi}^{[i]}}=\operatorname{softmax}(\mathbf{\psi}^{[i]})-\operatorname{sg}(\mathbf{r}^{[i]})=f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})-\operatorname{sg}(\mathbf{r}^{[i]}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">which is Eq. (<a href="#A3.E23" title="In Lemma 1. ‣ C.1 Analysis of Early-Learning Regularization in Eq. () ‣ Appendix C Disccusion ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>).
Applying the chain rule and averaging over <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> gives Eq. (<a href="#A3.E24" title="In Lemma 1. ‣ C.1 Analysis of Early-Learning Regularization in Eq. () ‣ Appendix C Disccusion ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>).
Replacing <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{sg}(\mathbf{r}^{[i]})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{sg}(\mathbf{r}^{[i]})</annotation></semantics></math> by its explicit form produces Eq. (<a href="#A3.E25" title="In Lemma 1. ‣ C.1 Analysis of Early-Learning Regularization in Eq. () ‣ Appendix C Disccusion ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark.</span> Eq. (<a href="#A3.E25" title="In Lemma 1. ‣ C.1 Analysis of Early-Learning Regularization in Eq. () ‣ Appendix C Disccusion ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>) shows that <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{Cls}}^{\mathrm{NL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Cls</mi><mi>NL</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{Cls}}^{\mathrm{NL}}</annotation></semantics></math> behaves like the standard cross-entropy gradient plus an ELR-like corrective term. This term amplifies gradients on clean samples and counteracts gradients on noisy samples. Specifically, we expand this ELR-like corrective term into:</p>
<table id="A3.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}^{[i]}_{y}:=\frac{f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})}{1-\langle f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]}),f_{\phi}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})\rangle}\sum_{k=1}^{c}(f_{\phi}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})_{k}-f_{\phi}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})_{y})f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})_{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>c</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}^{[i]}_{y}:=\frac{f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})}{1-\langle f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]}),f_{\phi}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})\rangle}\sum_{k=1}^{c}(f_{\phi}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})_{k}-f_{\phi}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})_{y})f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})_{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the true class, then the <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>th entry of <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})</annotation></semantics></math> tends to be dominant during early-learning.
In that case, the <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>th entry of <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}^{[i]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐠</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}^{[i]}</annotation></semantics></math> is negative.
This is useful both for examples with clean labels and for examples with noisy labels.
For examples with clean labels, the cross-entropy term <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})-\mathbf{\hat{y}}^{[i]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})-\mathbf{\hat{y}}^{[i]}</annotation></semantics></math> tends to vanish after the early-learning stage because <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}^{[i]})</annotation></semantics></math> is very close to <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\hat{y}}^{[i]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\hat{y}}^{[i]}</annotation></semantics></math>, allowing examples with wrong labels to dominant the gradient.
Adding <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}^{[i]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐠</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}^{[i]}</annotation></semantics></math> counteracts this effect by ensuring that the magnitudes of the coefficients on examples with clean labels remain large.
Thus, <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}^{[i]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐠</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}^{[i]}</annotation></semantics></math> fulfils the two desired properties that boosting the gradient of examples with clean labels, and neutralizing the gradient of the examples with false labels.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Class-Prior Estimation in Imprecise-Label Datasets</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When the class priors <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y)</annotation></semantics></math> (here we slightly abuse notation and denote them as <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>) are not directly accessible to the learning algorithm, they can be estimated using off-the-shelf estimation methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022a</a>)</cite>.
In this section, we present the problem formulation and outline how class priors can be estimated in practice.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">C.2.1 </span>Class-prior estimation in Partial-label datasets</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In partial-label learning, each instance is associated with a candidate label set rather than a single ground-truth label.
This label ambiguity makes it difficult to estimate the class prior distribution, since simply counting training samples per class is no longer feasible.
To address this issue, we adopt an iterative estimation strategy that updates the class prior in a moving-average manner.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We use the model’s predicted labels as a proxy for class prior estimation.
Since predictions in the early stage of training are often unreliable, we design a moving-average update rule that gradually stabilizes the estimated distribution.
The update starts from a uniform prior <math id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}=[1/c,\ldots,1/c]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}=[1/c,\ldots,1/c]</annotation></semantics></math>,
and is refined at each training epoch as</p>
<table id="A3.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}\leftarrow\mu\mathbf{r}+(1-\mu)\mathbf{z},\qquad\mathbf{z}_{j}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbb{I}\!\left(j=\arg\max_{y\in S_{i}}f_{j}(x_{i})\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐫</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.219em;" width="0.219em"><mi>𝕀</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}\leftarrow\mu\mathbf{r}+(1-\mu)\mathbf{z},\qquad\mathbf{z}_{j}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbb{I}\!\left(j=\arg\max_{y\in S_{i}}f_{j}(x_{i})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is a momentum parameter, <math id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the candidate label set for sample <math id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{j}(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{j}(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the model prediction for class <math id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.
This rule progressively refines <math id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> as the model improves, leading to more accurate and stable class-prior estimates.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">C.2.2 </span>Class-prior estimation in Supplementary-unlabeled datasets</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the case of supplementary-unlabeled datasets, which also is called semi-supervised datasets, the estimation of class-prior is relatively straightforward.
We assume that the distribution of the labeled dataset is consistent with that of the unlabeled dataset.
Therefore, the class-prior can be directly obtained by counting the class distribution over the labeled dataset,
which serves as a reliable approximation of the overall data distribution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">C.2.3 </span>Class-Prior Estimation in Noisy-Label Datasets</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the widely adopted class-dependent label noise setting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib102" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2020</a>)</cite>,
where the observed noisy label of each <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on its underlying clean label.
Formally, the transition probability from class <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to class <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="A3.Ex71" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\widetilde{Y}=e_{j}\!\mid\!Y=e_{i},X=\mathbf{x})=P(\widetilde{Y}=e_{j}\!\mid\!Y=e_{i})=T_{ij},\quad\forall i,j\in[[c]]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∣</mo></mpadded><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∣</mo></mpadded><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\widetilde{Y}=e_{j}\!\mid\!Y=e_{i},X=\mathbf{x})=P(\widetilde{Y}=e_{j}\!\mid\!Y=e_{i})=T_{ij},\quad\forall i,j\in[[c]],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}=[T_{ij}]\in[0,1]^{c\times c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}=[T_{ij}]\in[0,1]^{c\times c}</annotation></semantics></math> is the noise transition matrix.
To make the estimation of <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> feasible, we follow prior work and impose the following assumptions.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmassumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Sufficiently Scattered Assumption <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2021b</a>)</cite>)<span id="Thmassumption1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The clean class posterior <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y\!\mid\!X)=[P(Y=e_{1}\!\mid\!X),\ldots,P(Y=e_{c}\!\mid\!X)]^{\top}\in[0,1]^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mpadded style="width:0.633em;" width="0.633em"><mi>Y</mi></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true">∣</mo></mpadded><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∣</mo></mpadded><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∣</mo></mpadded><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y\!\mid\!X)=[P(Y=e_{1}\!\mid\!X),\ldots,P(Y=e_{c}\!\mid\!X)]^{\top}\in[0,1]^{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be sufficiently scattered if there exists a set
<math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}=\{\mathbf{x}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{x}_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}=\{\mathbf{x}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{x}_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the matrix <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}=[P(Y\!\mid\!X=\mathbf{x}_{1}),\ldots,P(Y\!\mid\!X=\mathbf{x}_{m})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mpadded style="width:0.633em;" width="0.633em"><mi>Y</mi></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true">∣</mo></mpadded><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mpadded style="width:0.633em;" width="0.633em"><mi>Y</mi></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true">∣</mo></mpadded><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}=[P(Y\!\mid\!X=\mathbf{x}_{1}),\ldots,P(Y\!\mid\!X=\mathbf{x}_{m})]</annotation></semantics></math>
satisfies:
(i) <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}\subseteq\mathrm{cone}\{\mathbf{H}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>cone</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}\subseteq\mathrm{cone}\{\mathbf{H}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}=\{\mathbf{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{c}\!\mid\!\mathbf{v}^{\top}\mathbf{1}\geq\sqrt{c-1}\|\mathbf{v}\|_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.052em;" width="0.052em"><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∣</mo></mpadded><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}=\{\mathbf{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{c}\!\mid\!\mathbf{v}^{\top}\mathbf{1}\geq\sqrt{c-1}\|\mathbf{v}\|_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{cone}\{\mathbf{H}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cone</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cone}\{\mathbf{H}\}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the convex cone generated by the columns of <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}</annotation></semantics></math>;
(ii) <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{cone}\{\mathbf{H}\}\nsubseteq\mathrm{cone}\{\mathbf{U}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>cone</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊈</mo><mrow><mi>cone</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐔</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cone}\{\mathbf{H}\}\nsubseteq\mathrm{cone}\{\mathbf{U}\}</annotation></semantics></math> for any unitary matrix <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{c\times c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{c\times c}</annotation></semantics></math> that is not a permutation matrix.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span><span id="Thmassumption2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Nonsingular <math id="Thmassumption2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmassumption2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The transition matrix <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonsingular, i.e., <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Rank}(\mathbf{T})=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Rank}(\mathbf{T})=c</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Sufficiently Scattered Assumption ( , )). ‣ C.2.3 Class-Prior Estimation in Noisy-Label Datasets ‣ C.2 Class-Prior Estimation in Imprecise-Label Datasets ‣ Appendix C Disccusion ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> ensures that the clean posteriors are sufficiently scattered so that the ground-truth <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> can be identified,
while Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 (Nonsingular 𝐓). ‣ C.2.3 Class-Prior Estimation in Noisy-Label Datasets ‣ C.2 Class-Prior Estimation in Imprecise-Label Datasets ‣ Appendix C Disccusion ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> guarantees the invertibility of <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> denote the noise rate.
For symmetric label noise, we have <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{ii}=1-\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{ii}=1-\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{ij}=\frac{\epsilon}{c-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{ij}=\frac{\epsilon}{c-1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq i." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq i.</annotation></semantics></math>
In practice, the transition matrix can be estimated by solving the following optimization problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2021b</a>)</cite>:</p>
<table id="A3.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E28.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\min_{\theta,\widehat{\mathbf{T}}}L(\theta,\widehat{\mathbf{T}})=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\ell\big(\widehat{\mathbf{T}}^{\top}h_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{i}),\widetilde{y}_{i}\big)+\lambda\cdot\log\det(\widehat{\mathbf{T}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></munder><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\theta,\widehat{\mathbf{T}}}L(\theta,\widehat{\mathbf{T}})=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\ell\big(\widehat{\mathbf{T}}^{\top}h_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{i}),\widetilde{y}_{i}\big)+\lambda\cdot\log\det(\widehat{\mathbf{T}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> is a loss function (typically cross-entropy),
<math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\theta}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\theta}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the output of a neural network parameterized by <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the regularization term <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\log\det(\widehat{\mathbf{T}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\det(\widehat{\mathbf{T}})</annotation></semantics></math> encourages the estimated transition matrix to have minimal simplex volume.
Here <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a trade-off hyperparameter.
By Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Sufficiently Scattered Assumption ( , )). ‣ C.2.3 Class-Prior Estimation in Noisy-Label Datasets ‣ C.2 Class-Prior Estimation in Imprecise-Label Datasets ‣ Appendix C Disccusion ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the solution <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathbf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathbf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> converges to the true <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> given sufficient noisy data (Theorem 1 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2021b</a>)</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Once the transition matrix <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is estimated,
the clean class prior <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=[\pi_{1},\ldots,\pi_{c}]^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=[\pi_{1},\ldots,\pi_{c}]^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> can be obtained by solving the following system of linear equations:</p>
<table id="A3.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E29.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{\begin{aligned} \widetilde{\pi}_{1}&amp;=T_{11}\pi_{1}+T_{21}\pi_{2}+\cdots+T_{c1}\pi_{c}\\
\widetilde{\pi}_{2}&amp;=T_{12}\pi_{1}+T_{22}\pi_{2}+\cdots+T_{c2}\pi_{c}\\
&amp;\;\;\vdots\\
\widetilde{\pi}_{c}&amp;=T_{1c}\pi_{1}+T_{2c}\pi_{2}+\cdots+T_{cc}\pi_{c}\end{aligned},\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">⋮</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\begin{aligned} \widetilde{\pi}_{1}&amp;=T_{11}\pi_{1}+T_{21}\pi_{2}+\cdots+T_{c1}\pi_{c}\\
\widetilde{\pi}_{2}&amp;=T_{12}\pi_{1}+T_{22}\pi_{2}+\cdots+T_{c2}\pi_{c}\\
&amp;\;\;\vdots\\
\widetilde{\pi}_{c}&amp;=T_{1c}\pi_{1}+T_{2c}\pi_{2}+\cdots+T_{cc}\pi_{c}\end{aligned},\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\pi}_{i}=P(\widetilde{Y}=e_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\pi}_{i}=P(\widetilde{Y}=e_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the noisy class prior of the <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th class.
The empirical estimate of <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\pi}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\pi}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed as</p>
<table id="A3.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E30.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{\widetilde{\pi}}_{i}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\mathbf{1}\{\widetilde{y}_{j}=e_{i}\},\quad\forall i\in[[c]]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\widetilde{\pi}}_{i}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\mathbf{1}\{\widetilde{y}_{j}=e_{i}\},\quad\forall i\in[[c]].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Solving this system yields the clean class prior <math id="A3.SS2.SSS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, which is then used in subsequent modeling.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Implementation Details</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our implementation is based on PyTorch 1.12 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paszke et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, and all experiments were conducted on NVIDIA Tesla A100 GPUs with CUDA 12.4.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Imprecise-label construction.</span>
For all class-dependent partial-label datasets, we construct a <math id="A4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\times 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\times 10</annotation></semantics></math> circulant transition matrix
<math id="A4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;q\!+\!0.2&amp;q&amp;q-0.2&amp;\cdots&amp;q\!+\!0.2&amp;q&amp;q\!-\!0.2\\
q\!-\!0.2&amp;1&amp;q\!+\!0.2&amp;q&amp;\cdots&amp;q&amp;q\!-\!0.2&amp;q\!+\!0.2\\
q&amp;q\!-\!0.2&amp;1&amp;q\!+\!0.2&amp;\cdots&amp;q\!-\!0.2&amp;q\!+\!0.2&amp;q\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots\\
q\!+\!0.2&amp;q&amp;q-0.2&amp;1&amp;\cdots&amp;q&amp;q-0.2&amp;1\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">+</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mi>q</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">+</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mi>q</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">+</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mi>q</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>q</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">+</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>q</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">+</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">+</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mi>q</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">+</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mi>q</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>q</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;q\!+\!0.2&amp;q&amp;q-0.2&amp;\cdots&amp;q\!+\!0.2&amp;q&amp;q\!-\!0.2\\
q\!-\!0.2&amp;1&amp;q\!+\!0.2&amp;q&amp;\cdots&amp;q&amp;q\!-\!0.2&amp;q\!+\!0.2\\
q&amp;q\!-\!0.2&amp;1&amp;q\!+\!0.2&amp;\cdots&amp;q\!-\!0.2&amp;q\!+\!0.2&amp;q\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots\\
q\!+\!0.2&amp;q&amp;q-0.2&amp;1&amp;\cdots&amp;q&amp;q-0.2&amp;1\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each row maps a true label to a candidate set of labels with varying probabilities, and <math id="A4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is set to <math id="A4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math>.
For noisy-label datasets with asymmetric noise (40% flip probability), we adopt the following mappings:
<span id="A4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fashion-MNIST:</span> ‘Pullover’<math id="A4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>‘Sneaker’, ‘Dress’<math id="A4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>‘Bag’, ‘Sandal’<math id="A4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>‘Shirt’, ‘Shirt’<math id="A4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>‘Sandal’.
<span id="A4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CIFAR-10:</span> ‘Truck’<math id="A4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>‘Automobile’, ‘Bird’<math id="A4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>‘Airplane’, ‘Deer’<math id="A4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>‘Horse’, ‘Cat’<math id="A4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>‘Dog’, ‘Dog’<math id="A4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>‘Cat’.
<span id="A4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ImageNette:</span> ‘Tench’<math id="A4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>‘English springer’, ‘Cassette player’<math id="A4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>‘Garbage truck’, ‘Chain saw’<math id="A4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>‘Church’, ‘Golf ball’<math id="A4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>‘Parachute’, ‘Parachute’<math id="A4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>‘Golf ball’.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model setup.</span>
The overall diffusion framework follows <span id="A4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Edm</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and the training hyperparameters are kept consistent with those reported therein.
For all experiments, we adopt the DDPM++ network architecture with a U-Net backbone.
Specifically, we employ the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of <math id="A4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\mathrm{e}{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\mathrm{e}{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>, parameters <math id="A4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta_{1},\beta_{2})=(0.9,0.999)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.999</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta_{1},\beta_{2})=(0.9,0.999)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=1\mathrm{e}{-8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=1\mathrm{e}{-8}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The EMA decay is set to <math id="A4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math>.
We use a batch size of 128 for Fashion-MNIST, 64 for CIFAR-10, and 16 for ImageNette.
For the diffusion classifier, we set the timestep interval length <math id="A4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> to 6.4.
All models are trained from scratch for 200k iterations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the trained CDMs using four unconditional metrics, including Fréchet Inception Distance (FID) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Heusel et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, Inception Score (IS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Salimans et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, Density, and Coverage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Naeem et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, and three conditional metrics, namely CW-FID, CW-Density, CW-Coverage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
All metrics are computed using the official implementation of DLSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
Although these metrics have been introduced in related work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Na et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, we briefly recap them here for completeness and clarity.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unconditional metrics.</span>
Unconditional metrics evaluate generated samples without reference to class labels.
In our experiments, images are first generated conditionally per class but then pooled without labels when computing the metrics. This evaluation protocol is consistent with prior studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaneko et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="A5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">FID measures the distance between real and generated image distributions in the pre-trained feature space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Szegedy et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, indicating the fidelity and diversity of generated images.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">IS evaluates whether generated images belong to distinct classes and whether each image is class-consistent, reflecting the realism and class separability of generated images.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Density and Coverage are reliable versions of Precision and Recall <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Naeem et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, respectively. Density measures how well generated samples cover real data distribution, while Coverage assesses how well real samples are represented by generated ones.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conditional metrics.</span>
To measure conditional consistency, we adopt class-wise (CW) variants of the above metrics, which compute each metric separately within each class and then average across classes.
Notably, CW-FID (also called intra-FID) is widely used in conditional generative modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Miyato &amp; Koyama, 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaneko et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, and has been highlighted as a key measure of conditional distribution quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Remark</span>: It should be noted that the Fashion-MNIST dataset is not suitable for evaluation using these metrics, so we do not perform evaluation on the Fahsion-MNIST dataset.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Full Results in Weakly Supervised Learning</h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building on the experiments presented in the main text, we further provide an extended comparison with a broader set of methods to ensure a comprehensive evaluation. The details are summarized as</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Partial-label learning.</span>
We compare against ten representative baselines: <span id="A5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">PRODEN</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lv et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, <span id="A5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">CAVL</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib108" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2021b</a>)</cite>, <span id="A5.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">POP</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, <span id="A5.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">CC</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, <span id="A5.SS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">LWS</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, <span id="A5.SS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">IDGP</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, <span id="A5.SS2.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">PiCO</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, <span id="A5.SS2.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">ABLE</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, <span id="A5.SS2.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">CRDPLL</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and <span id="A5.SS2.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">DIRK</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
For a fair comparison, we follow the hyperparameter settings used in <span id="A5.SS2.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">PLENCH</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.
The complete results are reported in Table <a href="#A5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ E.2 Full Results in Weakly Supervised Learning ‣ Appendix E Experiments ‣ Learning Robust Diffusion Models from Imprecise Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Semi-supervised learning.</span>
We follow the training and evaluation protocols of <span id="A5.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">USB</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022c</a>)</cite>, a widely adopted benchmark for fair and unified SSL comparisons.
Our baselines cover a broad spectrum of recent approaches.
First, we include confidence-thresholding methods such as <span id="A5.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">FixMatch</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sohn et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, <span id="A5.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">FlexMatch</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib107" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2021a</a>)</cite>, <span id="A5.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">FreeMatch</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022d</a>)</cite>, <span id="A5.SS2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">ReMixMatch</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Berthelot et al., 2019a</a>)</cite>, <span id="A5.SS2.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">Dash</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and <span id="A5.SS2.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">UDA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.
Second, we consider contrastive-learning based and pseudo-label based methods, including <span id="A5.SS2.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">CoMatch</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2021a</a>)</cite>, <span id="A5.SS2.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">SoftMatch</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and <span id="A5.SS2.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">SimMatch</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib116" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
Finally, we add several classical and widely studied SSL approaches, including <span id="A5.SS2.p3.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">Pseudo-Labeling</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2013</a>)</cite>, <span id="A5.SS2.p3.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">VAT</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Miyato et al., 2018</a>)</cite> and <span id="A5.SS2.p3.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">Mean Teacher</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tarvainen &amp; Valpola, 2017</a>)</cite>.
This diverse collection of baselines allows us to rigorously examine whether our framework remains competitive against both state-of-the-art and classical SSL methods under consistent experimental setups.</p>
</div>
