<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="A6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.1 </span>Robust Diffusion Models</h3>

<div id="A6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training robust conditional diffusion models under limited or imprecise supervision remains a relatively underexplored direction.
Recent works have started to tackle this challenge by tailoring diffusion models to specific forms of weak or noisy information.
For example, noise-robust diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Na et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite> focus on mitigating the effect of corrupted labels during training; positive-unlabeled diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Takahashi et al., 2025</a>)</cite> leverage positive samples along with large amounts of unlabeled data to approximate conditional distributions; and semi-supervised diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Givens et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> models aim to utilize both labeled and unlabeled data to improve conditional generation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.2 </span>Imprecise Label Learning</h3>

<div id="A6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Imprecise label learning aims to address scenarios where the supervision signals are incomplete, ambiguous, or corrupted, thereby deviating from clean ground-truth labels.
Existing studies can be broadly categorized according to the type of imprecision present in the dataset, including partial-label learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lv et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, where each instance is associated with a candidate set of labels containing the true label; semi-supervised learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Berthelot et al., 2019b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib107" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2021a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rasmus et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib116" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, where only a small subset of samples are labeled while the majority remain unlabeled; and noisy-label learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goldberger &amp; Ben-Reuven, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, where the observed labels are corrupted versions of the true labels.
Beyond these canonical settings, there is also growing interest in mixture imprecise-label learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib110" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shukla et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which combines multiple forms of imprecision within a unified framework to better reflect the complexity of real-world data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.3 </span>Dataset Condensation</h3>

<div id="A6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Dataset distillation (DD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2018</a>)</cite> compresses a large labeled dataset into a compact synthetic set that preserves task-relevant information, thereby reducing training cost while retaining accuracy comparable to training on the full dataset. Approaches include <em id="A6.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bi-level optimization</em>, which learns synthetic data whose training signals emulate those of the original data via <em id="A6.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">gradient matching</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib114" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, <em id="A6.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">trajectory matching</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cazenavette et al., 2022a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cui et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and <em id="A6.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">meta-model matching</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loo et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib117" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>)</cite>; these methods often achieve high fidelity but incur notable computational and memory costs. A second stream, <em id="A6.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">distribution matching</em>, aligns pixel-, feature-, or kernel-space statistics between synthetic and original data to improve efficiency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sajedi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shin et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xue et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Complementing these, <em id="A6.SS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">decoupled optimization</em> increases efficiency by removing the inner–outer dependency of the bi-level framework, for example, by matching running statistics embedded in a pretrained model or by leveraging pretrained models to select class-preserving crops from the original dataset. Representative methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib105" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>)</cite> have scaled DD to large-scale settings and motivated follow-ups that broaden feature-statistic alignment and stabilize optimization protocols <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib104" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin &amp; Shen, 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="A7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A limitation of our work is that the EDM used are relatively small.
While they provide a clear formulation and allow for a principled analysis, exploring larger-scale diffusion models such as stable diffusion is left for future work.
Moreover, our study focuses on three widely used weakly supervised learning settings.
Extending our framework to other paradigms, such as positive-unlabeled learning or multiple-instance learning, would be an interesting direction to further broaden the impact of generative models in weakly supervised learning.</p>
</div>
